# TypeScript Conventions

## Types & Interfaces

- Use `interface` for object shapes, `type` for unions/intersections/mapped types
- **No `I` prefix** — use `Props`, `Session`, `AgentModel`, not `IProps`, `ISession`
- Props interfaces are defined in the component file, not in separate type files
- Generated types from `types/generated/` are the source of truth for backend models
- Avoid `any` — prefer `unknown` with type guards
- Use `as const` for literal tuples and config objects

## Naming

- PascalCase: types, interfaces, components, pages
- camelCase: variables, functions, props
- UPPER_CASE: module-level constants (`AGENT_FILTER_OPTIONS`, `STATE_CONFIG`)

## Patterns

```tsx
// Props — plain interface, no I prefix
interface Props {
  sessions: Session[];
  cableStream?: string;
}

// Discriminated unions for state
type RequestState =
  | { status: 'idle' }
  | { status: 'loading' }
  | { status: 'success'; data: Session }
  | { status: 'error'; error: string };

// Prefer explicit return types for exported functions
export function formatTokens(n: number): string { ... }
```

## Anti-Patterns

```tsx
// ❌ any
const data: any = response;

// ❌ I prefix
interface ISessionProps { ... }

// ❌ Enum (use union instead)
enum Status { Active, Inactive }
// ✅
type Status = 'active' | 'inactive';
```
